        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "An AVL tree is more strictly balanced: about one point four four log n tall, against two log n for red-black. So lookups touch slightly fewer nodes. But keeping that balance costs more on writes. A delete can rotate at every level, and an AVL tree updates heights all the way up. A red-black tree does at most two rotations per insert, and three per delete, and much of the rest is recoloring. That's why most standard libraries use red-black trees: std map and set, Java's TreeMap, and the Linux kernel's rbtree.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Tighter, or <em>cheaper</em> to fix",
            "left": {
              "at": "strictly",
